## 📜 Part A — AI Governance Board Charter (one page)

**Purpose.** Provide enterprise oversight of AI systems so they are lawful, safe, fair, and aligned with organizational values — enabling responsible adoption at speed, not blocking it.

**Scope.** All AI/ML systems — built, bought, or embedded — across their lifecycle, including third-party GenAI and shadow AI once discovered.

**Authority.** The board can approve, approve-with-conditions, pause, or reject any AI system in scope. Its decisions on Tier 1 (high-risk) systems are binding.

**Membership (chair + core voting members):**

| Seat | Role |
|------|------|
| **Chair** | VP / Head of AI Governance (accountable owner) |
| Security | CISO delegate |
| Legal / Privacy | DPO or legal counsel |
| Data | Head of Data / CDO delegate |
| Business | Rotating product/business sponsor |
| Ethics/RAI | Responsible AI lead |
| *Consulted as needed* | Engineering leads, HR, procurement, comms |

**Quorum:** Chair + Security + Legal + one business seat.

**Mandate boundaries:** The board governs *risk and compliance*, not product roadmaps. It does not design solutions; it sets guardrails and decides on exceptions.

---

## 🗳️ Part B — Decision Rights (Delegation of Authority)

The #1 failure of AI governance boards is becoming a bottleneck — every decision escalates and the queue kills adoption. Push decisions to the lowest safe level:

| Decision | Owner can decide | AI Gov Lead decides | Board decides |
|----------|:----------------:|:-------------------:|:-------------:|
| Tier 3 (minimal-risk) go-live | ✅ (self-attest) | Notified | — |
| Tier 2 (limited-risk) go-live | Recommends | ✅ | Informed |
| Tier 1 (high-risk) go-live | Recommends | Recommends | ✅ |
| Accept a Moderate residual risk | ✅ | Notified | — |
| Accept a High residual risk | — | ✅ | Informed |
| Accept a Critical residual risk | — | — | ✅ |
| Policy / standard changes | — | Recommends | ✅ |
| SEV-1 incident: halt a system | Any IRT member (emergency) | Confirms | Ratifies |
| Prohibited-use exception | — | — | ✅ (+ legal) |

> **Principle:** the board's calendar should be spent on Tier 1 systems, Critical risks, and policy — everything else is delegated with visibility.

